Stego_RSA Beginner CTF Writeup

Challenge Name: Stego_RSA
Category: Crypto
Solved Flag: picoCTF{rs4_k3y_1n_1mg_0a64c2f9}

Files Given:
1. image.jpg
2. flag.enc


1. First Observation

At first, I checked the file types:

file flag.enc
file image.jpg

The flag.enc file showed:

flag.enc: data

This means the file is just raw/binary data. It was not readable text.

When I tried:

cat flag.enc

the output was unreadable binary characters.

This was expected because .enc usually means “encrypted”. So I understood that flag.enc was probably an encrypted version of the flag.


2. Small Mistake I Made

I tried:

xxd flag

But this gave an error:

xxd: flag: No such file or directory

The mistake was simple: the file was not named “flag”. It was named “flag.enc”.

So the correct command was:

xxd flag.enc

This showed the encrypted file in hexadecimal form.

At this point, I could see the encrypted data, but I still could not decrypt it because I did not have the key yet.


3. Checking the Image

Since the challenge name was Stego_RSA, I knew there were probably two parts:

Stego = something hidden inside the image
RSA   = encryption/decryption using RSA

So I checked the image:

file image.jpg

The output showed something very important:

comment: "2d2d2d2d2d424547494e2050524956415445204b45592d2d2d2d2d..."

This was suspicious because the image had a JPEG comment section containing a long hex string.

This means we did use the image, but not by looking at the picture visually. We used the image as a container. The secret data was hidden inside the JPEG metadata/comment.


4. Decoding the Hidden Hex

The hex string started with:

2d2d2d2d2d424547494e2050524956415445204b45592d2d2d2d2d

After decoding it from hex, it became:

-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----

This was the turning point.

It meant the image was hiding a private key inside its metadata.


5. Why We Thought It Was RSA

There were several clues that told us this was RSA:

First, the challenge name was Stego_RSA.

Second, the file flag.enc looked like encrypted binary data.

Third, the image contained a private key.

The private key started like this:

-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----
MIIEvAIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKY...

Even though the header said:

-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----

instead of:

-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----

it could still be an RSA private key.

This is because there are different PEM key formats.

“BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY” is a traditional RSA-specific format.

“BEGIN PRIVATE KEY” is usually PKCS#8 format. In this format, the key header is generic, and the algorithm information is stored inside the key data.

So the key did not directly say RSA in the header, but OpenSSL could identify it as an RSA private key.


6. Extracting the Hidden Private Key

To extract the JPEG comment properly, I used:

exiftool -b -Comment image.jpg > key.hex

This saved the hidden comment into a file called key.hex.

Then I converted the hex into the real private key file:

cat key.hex | tr -d '\n\r ' | xxd -r -p > private.pem

Explanation:

exiftool -b -Comment image.jpg
extracts only the raw JPEG comment.

tr -d '\n\r '
removes extra spaces or new lines.

xxd -r -p
converts hexadecimal back into normal text/binary.

private.pem
is the final private key file.


7. Confirming the Private Key

I checked the private key with:

cat private.pem | head

It showed:

-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----
MIIEvAIBADANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAASCBKY...

That confirmed that the hidden hex was successfully converted into a PEM private key.

I could also verify it with:

openssl pkey -in private.pem -text -noout

This would show RSA key details like modulus, public exponent, private exponent, primes, etc.


8. Decrypting the Encrypted Flag

Now I had:

flag.enc     = encrypted flag
private.pem  = RSA private key

So I used OpenSSL to decrypt the file:

openssl pkeyutl -decrypt -inkey private.pem -in flag.enc -out flag.txt -pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:pkcs1

Then I read the output:

cat flag.txt

The decrypted flag was:

picoCTF{rs4_k3y_1n_1mg_0a64c2f9}


9. Why This Was Tagged Crypto Even Though It Felt Like Stego

The challenge was tagged crypto because the final flag was encrypted using RSA.

But the RSA private key was hidden inside the image metadata, so the first part felt like steganography.

The full flow was:

Stego part:
image.jpg → JPEG comment → hex → private.pem

Crypto part:
private.pem + flag.enc → RSA decrypt → flag

So this was actually a mixed challenge.

It used steganography to hide the RSA key, and cryptography to encrypt the flag.


10. Main Concept Learned

The most important lesson:

If the RSA private key is leaked, RSA encryption becomes useless.

In RSA:

Public key  = used to encrypt
Private key = used to decrypt

So if someone hides or accidentally leaks the private key, anyone who finds it can decrypt the encrypted data.

In this challenge, the private key was hidden inside the image comment, so once I found it, the RSA encryption was broken.


11. Commands Used

file flag.enc
file image.jpg

cat flag.enc

xxd flag.enc

exiftool -b -Comment image.jpg > key.hex

cat key.hex | tr -d '\n\r ' | xxd -r -p > private.pem

cat private.pem | head

openssl pkey -in private.pem -text -noout

openssl pkeyutl -decrypt -inkey private.pem -in flag.enc -out flag.txt -pkeyopt rsa_padding_mode:pkcs1

cat flag.txt


12. Final Summary

At first, flag.enc was unreadable because it was encrypted.

Then I checked image.jpg and found a suspicious hex string inside the JPEG comment.

After decoding the hex, I found a private key.

Although the key header said “BEGIN PRIVATE KEY”, it was still an RSA private key in PKCS#8 format.

Using that private key, I decrypted flag.enc and got the flag.

Final flag:

picoCTF{rs4_k3y_1n_1mg_0a64c2f9}


Final takeaway:

Always check metadata in stego-style challenges.
Always check file names and extensions carefully.
If a private key is leaked, encrypted RSA data can be decrypted.
Crypto and stego can appear together in one challenge.
